First, I will start with Access Control.
Access Control is used to control who can access a resource and what they are allowed to do.
There are different access control models:
DAC (Discretionary Access Control)
The owner of a resource can give permissions to other users.
For example, the owner can allow someone to view, edit, or access a file.
MAC (Mandatory Access Control)
Access is controlled by strict rules and security policies.
Users cannot change the permissions themselves.
RBAC (Role-Based Access Control)
Access is based on a user's role.
For example, a person in the accounting department may have access to accounting information but cannot access the manager's account.
Rule-Based Access Control
Access is controlled by rules.
For example, an administrator can create rules to allow or deny access in a network.
ACL (Access Control List)
An ACL is a list of rules that allows or denies access.
For example:
Allow traffic from 192.168.1.10
Deny traffic from 192.168.1.20
ACLs can be used on routers, switches, firewalls, and other network devices.
